===============================================================================
ENTERPRISE LINUX PACKAGE REPOSITORY CONFIGURATION GUIDE
===============================================================================

LOCATION: /etc/yum.repos.d/
PURPOSE:  Contains repository configuration files (.repo) used by package
          managers (YUM/DNF) to fetch, update, and manage software packages.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. CORE OPERATING SYSTEM REPOSITORIES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* appstream.repo (AppStream)
  Provides additional user-space applications, runtime environments, databases,
  web servers, and development languages.

* baseos.repo (BaseOS)
  Provides fundamental core operating system packages and low-level utilities
  required for basic system boot and administration.

* extras.repo (Extras)
  Contains supplemental packages and utilities that extend core operating
  system functionality.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. DEVELOPER & BUILD REPOSITORIES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* crb.repo (CodeReady Builder / CRB)
  Contains developer headers, libraries, and compilation tools needed for
  building custom software from source.

* plus.repo (Plus)
  Offers modified, enhanced, or alternative versions of standard system
  packages to suit specialized environment needs.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. ENTERPRISE & HIGH AVAILABILITY REPOSITORIES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* highavailability.repo (High Availability)
  Supplies software and utilities required to configure high-availability
  clustering and automatic system failover.

* resilientstorage.repo (Resilient Storage)
  Provides clustered file system and shared storage software for network and
  enterprise storage setups.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. SPECIALIZED WORKLOAD REPOSITORIES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* nfv.repo (Network Functions Virtualization)
  Provides network virtualization components and high-performance packet
  processing tools.
----
* rt.repo (Real-Time)
  Contains specialized real-time kernels and low-latency tuning utilities
  for time-sensitive applications.

* sap.repo & saphana.repo (SAP / SAP HANA)
  Contains pre-configured system tuning libraries, drivers, and runtime
  configurations required for running certified enterprise applications.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. STRUCTURE OF A .REPO FILE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each file uses an INI-style structure to define repositories:

[repository-id]
name=Repository Display Name
mirrorlist=https://example.com/mirrorlist?repo=BaseOS
enabled=1
gpgcheck=1
gpgkey=file:///etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-vendor

FIELD DEFINITIONS:
- [repository-id] : Unique internal identifier for the repository.
- name            : Human-readable description.
- mirrorlist/baseurl : Web URL pointing to the RPM package server.
- enabled         : Controls repository activation (1 = Enabled, 0 = Disabled).
- gpgcheck        : Enforces package cryptographic signature verification.
- gpgkey          : File path or URL to the vendor GPG key.

